Z||000012||fiero56||01-10-2001||04:51 PM||||Wondering how the struts are able to move independant of the towers, they seem to be solidly mounted to me.||||reg||1
Z||000013||william_thorniii||01-10-2001||06:43 PM||||56,<P>Technically the towers and the struts are rigidly mounted.  If you want to look at small enough deflections though there is relative movement between the two.  It should be assumed that the struts will carry more load than the towers and thus would be more prone to flexure.  I would also assume that there are bars that attach to the towers.  Eventually, the relative motion between the two could open up the through holes in the towers and cause serious problems.  It is highly unlikely (in my head) anyway.  <P><BR>KEV,<P>Strut braces or strut tower braces in no way have any bearing on a sway bar or its function in the vehicle.  <P>&lt;snip and edited&gt;  That previous statement is not true at all.  Strut bars increase the rigidity of the chassis overall.  If the chassis is flexing severly, an anti-roll bar cannot do it's job.  <P>&lt;Now we take you back to a posting previously in progres...&gt;  Sway bars or anti-roll bars act as springs in the system effectively transferring force from the inside wheel to the outside wheel on a corner.  This effectively increases your tire patch on the road.  As a by-product, it reduces body roll.  Strut bars or tower bars are simply a rigid member.  I do have doubts that a singular piece of round steel tube can add significant rigidity to the chassis though... but that is an entirely different topic.  <P>****DISCLAIMER****<P>My comments are not in any way an attempt to insinuate that Darrels products are not top notch.  I talked with him at length about providing me with aluminum suspension bushings.  Everything that I saw down at Ed's meet and the Nationals last year was impeccably made.<P>William T. Thorn, III<p>[This message has been edited by william_thorniii (edited 01-10-2001).]||||reg||12
