Z||000022||stimpy||01-11-2001||10:32 AM||||Damn, WT3, what did Chris do wrong this time? I know it's a tradition to slander, flame, and otherwise sarcastically knock vendors, but he was merely mentioned, not even quoted.  I guess I still have a hard time reconciling why people who rebuild junk Fieros or sell valve stem caps are revered by this forum, while people who actually engineer products (like Chris and Archie) get dumped on .||||reg||1
Z||000023||william_thorniii||01-11-2001||11:36 AM||||EC05,<P>I have no problems with folks that use strut tower bars.  I think that they look cool.  I am simply arguing the some points made by others... in a silly attempt to actually educate folks and counteract the "I saw it on the internet, therefore it must be true" tendency.  <P>Stimpy,<P>My comments were not an attmpt to insinuate that Chris does not make good products.  My comments were made trying to point out the fact that the modifications he might have or might not have made to his suspension should not be a suspension bible.  If there were such a thing, I would prefer to take a gander at Formula 1.  Afterall it is undeniably the most technological form of automobile construction.  <P>Also, we do not know what Mr. West's intentions are or were for this car.  It serves us no point to make the statement that his car corners well.  I could make a statement that my father's 1924 Model T corners well.  A comment such as that is not a quantitative comment and therefore should be used to argue points.  Much like Travis stated, we would need solid cornering numbers to determine if his suspension is superior to anyone elses.  <P>KEV,<P>My point was only that one representing a company, individual or what-ever should understand their products before entering in to an argument about their products validity.  If you would like me to look up some excerpts to support my arguments and send them to you, I would be happy to do so.  The simple facts are as follows:<P>A strut tower bar increases the overall rigidity of the structure.  A sway bar increases a the loading of the inside tire during a corner thus increasing the contact patch.  It is a true statement that with a flimsy structure,a sway bar will not be allowed to transfer load between suspension components efficiently.  Therefore in some instances a strut tower bar can second handedly improve cornering by providing rigidity to much needed structures of many cars.  It can neither be said that a strut tower bar directly affects cornering nor it is significant on every type of vehicle.   <P>- Not really arguing... just stating facts.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
